WEBVTT

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:07.919
Here is our destination. Choose the Legendre degree n. The index j runs from zero to n and selects one term.

00:00:08.199 --> 00:00:20.437
Fix the radius rho. J sends the complex circle point z to omega on the ellipse boundary. Each term has coefficient alpha sub n comma j and exponent n minus twice j.

00:00:20.717 --> 00:00:31.386
Where does that coefficient come from? The variable s counts degree in the Legendre generating function. At omega equals J of z, it splits into two inverse square roots.

00:00:31.666 --> 00:00:40.766
Expand each factor using the binomial series. The first coefficients are one, one half, three eighths, and five sixteenths.

00:00:41.046 --> 00:01:02.072
Why these numbers? The binomial rule gives the product of the first ell odd numbers, divided by two to the power ell times ell factorial. Multiply top and bottom by the missing even factors. This gives the central binomial coefficient divided by four to the power ell. For ell equal to two, this gives three eighths.

00:01:03.046 --> 00:01:15.135
For s to the power n, choose degree n minus j from the first factor and j from the second. Multiply their coefficients. The powers of z subtract to give n minus twice j.

00:01:15.415 --> 00:01:25.095
At degree three, choose j equal to one. Three eighths times one half gives three sixteenths. The term is three sixteenths times z.

00:01:25.375 --> 00:01:34.471
Once n is fixed, write this coefficient as the weight w sub j. It is the same number. This gives the weighted sum of rotating powers.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:40.327
Take a complex point z on a circle of radius rho, at angle theta.

00:01:40.607 --> 00:01:44.601
The Joukowski map sends z to omega on the ellipse.

00:01:44.881 --> 00:01:50.589
The pullback expresses P sub n at omega as a function of the original circle angle.

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:56.964
In the cubic term, five sixteenths is the Laurent coefficient, and three is the exponent.

00:01:57.244 --> 00:02:04.633
At fixed radius, z cubed is rho cubed times the Fourier mode, e to the power three i theta.

00:02:04.913 --> 00:02:12.100
So the Fourier coefficient is five sixteenths times rho cubed, and the mode makes three turns per circuit.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:19.765
Fix the circle radius. Omega is the point on the boundary of the ellipse where we evaluate the polynomial.

00:02:20.045 --> 00:02:27.578
In the generating function, s is a separate variable. Its exponent tells us which Legendre polynomial to read.

00:02:27.858 --> 00:02:37.786
Look beside s squared. Its coefficient is P two of omega. Move the point along the ellipse and that value changes, while the power of s stays two.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.838
The auxiliary variable s keeps track of the Legendre degree.

00:02:43.118 --> 00:02:51.813
Choose s to the power p from the first factor, and s to the power q from the second. Their product is s to the power p plus q.

00:02:52.093 --> 00:02:59.002
For example, s squared times s gives s cubed, so this pair contributes to degree three.

00:02:59.282 --> 00:03:05.093
Its power of z is p minus q. Here, two minus one equals one.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.284
One Legendre value has two descriptions.

00:03:08.564 --> 00:03:12.372
We can evaluate the polynomial at a point on the ellipse.

00:03:12.652 --> 00:03:16.089
Or we can add a finite set of rotating arrows.

00:03:16.369 --> 00:03:18.302
Their sum gives that same value.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.819
How can points on a circle trace an ellipse?

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:25.211
Follow the midpoint between a point and its reciprocal.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.375
Start with a point z on the outer circle.

00:03:29.685 --> 00:03:33.714
Its distance from the origin is rho, which we’ll set to one and a half.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.680
Now mark one over z on the inner circle.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:40.433
Join the two points and take their midpoint.

00:03:40.743 --> 00:03:43.309
That’s the Joukowski map, J of z.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.157
Turn z counterclockwise.

00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:49.645
Its reciprocal turns clockwise through the same angle.

00:03:49.955 --> 00:03:51.154
Watch their midpoint.

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:56.050
As z makes one full turn, the midpoint traces a Bernstein ellipse.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.892
Which products contribute to a single Legendre degree?

00:04:01.172 --> 00:04:06.118
Keep only the pairs whose two powers of the series variable add to that degree.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.884
To find P three at that midpoint,

00:04:09.024 --> 00:04:12.356
take the coefficient of s cubed in the generating function.

00:04:12.666 --> 00:04:15.651
Choose degree p from the first binomial factor

00:04:15.791 --> 00:04:17.902
and degree q from the second.

00:04:18.212 --> 00:04:20.973
We need p plus q to equal three.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:25.098
How does each surviving pair become a rotating arrow?

00:04:25.378 --> 00:04:27.433
The weight and radius set its length.

00:04:27.713 --> 00:04:30.712
The difference of the indices sets how it turns.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.931
Each choice gives a power of z.

00:04:34.241 --> 00:04:36.786
Read p minus q from left to right.

00:04:37.096 --> 00:04:40.275
Three, one, minus one, minus three.

00:04:40.585 --> 00:04:43.777
Their weights are five sixteenths, three sixteenths,

00:04:43.917 --> 00:04:46.808
three sixteenths, and five sixteenths.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.113
Turn z again and watch the four arrows.

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:53.961
For exponent k and weight w,

00:04:54.101 --> 00:04:57.162
the length is w times rho to the power k.

00:04:57.472 --> 00:05:00.174
The angle is k times the angle of z,

00:05:00.314 --> 00:05:02.994
so a negative exponent reverses the turn.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.053
For example, z cubed has the radius cubed and three times the angle.

00:05:09.363 --> 00:05:12.321
Now place the four weighted arrows head to tail.

00:05:12.631 --> 00:05:17.289
The tip lands at the value of the third Legendre polynomial at our midpoint.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:25.560
In this product array, p plus q selects the Legendre degree, and p minus q selects the power of z.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:32.714
At p equals three and q equals zero, five sixteenths times z cubed gives the blue arrow.

00:05:32.994 --> 00:05:39.380
At p equals two and q equals one, three sixteenths times z gives the mint arrow.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:46.494
At p equals one and q equals two, three sixteenths times z inverse gives the violet arrow.

00:05:46.774 --> 00:05:55.276
At p equals zero and q equals three, five sixteenths times z to the power minus three gives the orchid arrow.

00:05:55.556 --> 00:06:01.876
The gold arrow runs from the origin to the final tip and shows their sum, P three at J of z.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:06.150
What if we add one arrow for every Legendre degree?

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.200
The fourth is our whole four-arrow sum, multiplied by s cubed.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:16.000
Follow the tip in the zoom window as we add the first forty.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:21.150
The gold diamond marks the limit. The smaller arrows keep closing the gap.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:25.201
Why does swapping the two preimages preserve the answer?

00:06:25.481 --> 00:06:28.413
The powers reverse, and the paired weights match.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.242
Finally, swap z and its reciprocal.

00:06:32.552 --> 00:06:35.106
The four terms trade places in pairs.

00:06:35.416 --> 00:06:39.886
The midpoint stays put, and the arrows still add up to the same value.

